.TH DANBY-TDSE 1
.SH NAME
danby-tdse \- a wave packet meeting a barrier, solved as it happens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/tdse
.SH MODEL
Every other quantum model in
.IR danby (1)
draws an answer worked out in advance: a standing state, a steady beam,
or a formula evaluated along an axis.
.IR Danby-packet (1)
comes closest to motion and still only substitutes a known width at each
instant.  This one solves the time-dependent Schrodinger equation, step
by step, and draws the result as it appears.
.PP
With psi = u + iv, in atomic units,
.PP
.EX
du/dt = -(1/2) v'' + V v
dv/dt = +(1/2) u'' - V u
.EE
.PP
which is a two-field problem of the shape
.IR pde (2)'s
.B "equation hoc"
takes, with V written as a function of x.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The equation is not in the program.  It is the text of
.BR /lib/danby/tdse.pde ,
read at startup, so it can be changed without a compiler \(em and so
.IR tdsetest (1)
can run the same text rather than a copy of it.  The two sliders that
appear in it are overridden afterwards through the problem's own
.IR hoc (2)
variables, which is why the file needs no substitution.
.SS What to look for
.B The packet splits.
Not "some particles go through and some do not": one packet arrives at
the wall and two leave it, one carrying on and one coming back.  The
numbers along the top are what fraction of it went each way, and the
last of them is the total, which must not change.
.PP
Set the barrier above the packet's energy and watch anyway.  At the
defaults the energy is 8 and the wall is 10, so nothing classical would
get through at all, and about a fifth of it does.
.PP
Widen the barrier and transmission collapses \(em 0.45 at width 0.4,
0.13 at 0.8, 0.064 at 1.2 \(em which is the exponential that makes a
tunnelling microscope work.
.PP
.IR Danby-tunnel (1)
gives the same two numbers from a formula, without solving anything.
Comparing them is the point of
.IR tdsetest (1).
.SS The numerics, which are not optional
The step is STAGGERED: v is advanced using the u just computed, not the
one it started with.  The eigenvalues here are purely imaginary, so
advancing both fields together multiplies every mode by
sqrt(1 + (lambda h)^2) at every step, for any h however small.  That is
not a step-size problem and no substep count fixes it \(em a
simultaneous update cannot solve this equation at all.  Measured: over
sixty steps the staggered norm holds to five decimals while the
simultaneous one passes a thousandfold on its way to 4.6e19.
.PP
The mesh is 384 by 2 rather than 384 by 1, because
.IR pde (2)
wants at least two rows each way.  The rows are seeded identically and
the boundary is clamped, so the y part of the Laplacian is exactly zero
and the problem is honestly one-dimensional.
.SS Controls
Space pauses, period advances one step while paused, r restarts, and q
or Escape quits.  The four tracks along the bottom are dragged with
button 1; any of them restarts the run, since the barrier is in the
equations and the packet is an initial condition, and neither can be
changed part way through and still mean anything.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/danby/tdse.pde
The equation.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/tdse.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-tunnel (1),
.IR danby-packet (1),
.IR tdsetest (1),
.IR pde (2)
.SH BUGS
The edges reflect.  The boundary is clamped, so a packet that reaches
either end comes back rather than leaving, and after long enough the
returning pieces interfere with what is still in flight.  The run is
sized so that the interesting part happens first; an absorbing boundary
would be the fix and is not here.
.PP
The barrier sits where the spec file puts it and is not on a slider, so
the distance the packet travels before it arrives is fixed.
.PP
The two numbers along the top are the probability each side of the
barrier's centre, which is what "reflected" and "transmitted" mean only
once the packet has finished crossing.  While it is still arriving they
are simply where the wave happens to be, and they move about.
